    {
      "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-47h",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "47H Public interest conditional exemptions—research",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/freedom-of-information-act-1982#sch-4-sec-47h"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "freedom-of-information-act-1982.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "freedom-of-information-act-1982.pdf, p.106",
      "values:originalText": "A document is conditionally exempt if:\n(a) it contains information relating to research that is being, or is\nto be, undertaken by an officer of an agency specified in\nSchedule 4; and\n(b) disclosure of the information before the completion of the\nresearch would be likely unreasonably to expose the agency\nor officer to disadvantage.\n\n\nExempt documents  Part IV\nPublic interest conditional exemptions  Division 3\nSection 47J\nFreedom of Information Act 1982\nCompilation date: 01/01/2026\nNote:\nAccess must generally be given to a conditionally exempt document\nunless it would be contrary to the public interest (see section 11A).",
      "skos:definition": "A document is conditionally exempt if:\n(a) it contains information relating to research that is being, or is\nto be, undertaken by an officer of an agency specified in\nSchedule 4; and\n(b) disclosure of the information before the completion of the\nresearch would be likely unreasonably to expose the agency\nor officer to disadvantage.\n\n\nExempt documents  Part IV\nPublic interest conditional exemptions  Division 3\nSection 47J\nFreedom of Information Act 1982\nCompilation date: 01/01/2026\nNote:\nAccess must generally be given to a conditionally exempt document\nunless it would be contrary to the public interest (see section 11A).",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-47h-norm"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "675dd7f11d6e3e23c6f1239f01f34840bf024bba36469882cecff402a531d56d",
      "skos:note": "Conditional exemptions for research documents"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/freedom-of-information-act-1982#sch-4-sec-47h",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "47H Public interest conditional exemptions—research",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 106,
      "values:originalText": "A document is conditionally exempt if:\n(a) it contains information relating to research that is being, or is\nto be, undertaken by an officer of an agency specified in\nSchedule 4; and\n(b) disclosure of the information before the completion of the\nresearch would be likely unreasonably to expose the agency\nor officer to disadvantage.\n\n\nExempt documents  Part IV\nPublic interest conditional exemptions  Division 3\nSection 47J\nFreedom of Information Act 1982\nCompilation date: 01/01/2026\nNote:\nAccess must generally be given to a conditionally exempt document\nunless it would be contrary to the public interest (see section 11A)."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-47h-norm",
      "@type": "values:Undertaking",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-47h"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-47j",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "47J Public interest conditional exemptions—the economy",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/freedom-of-information-act-1982#sch-4-sec-47j"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "freedom-of-information-act-1982.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "freedom-of-information-act-1982.pdf, p.107",
      "values:originalText": "(1) A document is conditionally exempt if its disclosure under this Act\nwould, or could be reasonably expected to, have a substantial\nadverse effect on Australia’s economy by:\n(a) influencing a decision or action of a person or entity; or\n(b) giving a person (or class of persons) an undue benefit or\ndetriment, in relation to business carried on by the person (or\nclass), by providing premature knowledge of proposed or\npossible action or inaction of a person or entity.\nNote:\nA person includes a body corporate and a body politic (see\nsubsection 2C(1) of the Acts Interpretation Act 1901). Examples of a\nbody politic include the government of the Commonwealth, a State, a\nTerritory or a foreign country.\n(2) For the purposes of subsection (1), a substantial adverse effect on\nAustralia’s economy includes a substantial adverse effect on:\n(a) a particular sector of the economy; or\n(b) the economy of a particular region of Australia.\n(2A) For the purposes of paragraph (2)(b), Norfolk Island is taken to be\na region of Australia.\n(3) The documents to which subsection (1) applies include, but are not\nlimited to, documents containing matter relating to any of the\nfollowing:\n(a) currency or exchange rates;\n(b) interest rates;\n(c) taxes, including duties of customs or of excise;\n(d) the regulation or supervision of banking, insurance and other\nfinancial institutions;\n(e) proposals for expenditure;\n(f) foreign investment in Australia;\n(g) borrowings by the Commonwealth, a State or an authority of\nthe Commonwealth, of Norfolk Island or of a State.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) A document is conditionally exempt if its disclosure under this Act\nwould, or could be reasonably expected to, have a substantial\nadverse effect on Australia’s economy by:\n(a) influencing a decision or action of a person or entity; or\n(b) giving a person (or class of persons) an undue benefit or\ndetriment, in relation to business carried on by the person (or\nclass), by providing premature knowledge of proposed or\npossible action or inaction of a person or entity.\nNote:\nA person includes a body corporate and a body politic (see\nsubsection 2C(1) of the Acts Interpretation Act 1901). Examples of a\nbody politic include the government of the Commonwealth, a State, a\nTerritory or a foreign country.\n(2) For the purposes of subsection (1), a substantial adverse effect on\nAustralia’s economy includes a substantial adverse effect on:\n(a) a particular sector of the economy; or\n(b) the economy of a particular region of Australia.\n(2A) For the purposes of paragraph (2)(b), Norfolk Island is taken to be\na region of Australia.\n(3) The documents to which subsection (1) applies include, but are not\nlimited to, documents containing matter relating to any of the\nfollowing:\n(a) currency or exchange rates;\n(b) interest rates;\n(c) taxes, including duties of customs or of excise;\n(d) the regulation or supervision of banking, insurance and other\nfinancial institutions;\n(e) proposals for expenditure;\n(f) foreign investment in Australia;\n(g) borrowings by the Commonwealth, a State or an authority of\nthe Commonwealth, of Norfolk Island or of a State.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-47j-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-47j-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-47j-ss-2a"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-47j-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "19ac0dc2b8368aaf7969a0b742386ea3971bd87a7e1cc4e1a195c2818da4ed0b"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/freedom-of-information-act-1982#sch-4-sec-47j",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "47J Public interest conditional exemptions—the economy",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 107,
      "values:originalText": "(1) A document is conditionally exempt if its disclosure under this Act\nwould, or could be reasonably expected to, have a substantial\nadverse effect on Australia’s economy by:\n(a) influencing a decision or action of a person or entity; or\n(b) giving a person (or class of persons) an undue benefit or\ndetriment, in relation to business carried on by the person (or\nclass), by providing premature knowledge of proposed or\npossible action or inaction of a person or entity.\nNote:\nA person includes a body corporate and a body politic (see\nsubsection 2C(1) of the Acts Interpretation Act 1901). Examples of a\nbody politic include the government of the Commonwealth, a State, a\nTerritory or a foreign country.\n(2) For the purposes of subsection (1), a substantial adverse effect on\nAustralia’s economy includes a substantial adverse effect on:\n(a) a particular sector of the economy; or\n(b) the economy of a particular region of Australia.\n(2A) For the purposes of paragraph (2)(b), Norfolk Island is taken to be\na region of Australia.\n(3) The documents to which subsection (1) applies include, but are not\nlimited to, documents containing matter relating to any of the\nfollowing:\n(a) currency or exchange rates;\n(b) interest rates;\n(c) taxes, including duties of customs or of excise;\n(d) the regulation or supervision of banking, insurance and other\nfinancial institutions;\n(e) proposals for expenditure;\n(f) foreign investment in Australia;\n(g) borrowings by the Commonwealth, a State or an authority of\nthe Commonwealth, of Norfolk Island or of a State."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-47j-ss-1",
