    {
      "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-55h",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "55H Procedure in IC review—reference of questions of law to",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/freedom-of-information-act-1982#sch-4-sec-55h"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "freedom-of-information-act-1982.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "freedom-of-information-act-1982.pdf, p.145",
      "values:originalText": "Federal Court of Australia\n(1) The Information Commissioner may, at any time during an IC\nreview, refer a question of law arising in an IC review to the\nFederal Court of Australia for decision.\n(2) The Information Commissioner may refer the question of law:\n(a) on the application of a review party; or\n(b) on the Information Commissioner’s initiative.\n(3) The Federal Court of Australia has jurisdiction to hear and\ndetermine a question of law referred to it.\n(4) The jurisdiction of the Federal Court of Australia may be exercised\nby a single judge of the Court.\n(5) If a question of law is referred, the Information Commissioner\nmust not, for the purposes of the IC review:\n(a) give a decision to which the question is relevant before the\nFederal Court of Australia makes a decision in relation to the\nreference; or\n(b) proceed in a manner, or make a decision, that is inconsistent\nwith the opinion of the Federal Court of Australia on the\nquestion.",
      "skos:definition": "Federal Court of Australia\n(1) The Information Commissioner may, at any time during an IC\nreview, refer a question of law arising in an IC review to the\nFederal Court of Australia for decision.\n(2) The Information Commissioner may refer the question of law:\n(a) on the application of a review party; or\n(b) on the Information Commissioner’s initiative.\n(3) The Federal Court of Australia has jurisdiction to hear and\ndetermine a question of law referred to it.\n(4) The jurisdiction of the Federal Court of Australia may be exercised\nby a single judge of the Court.\n(5) If a question of law is referred, the Information Commissioner\nmust not, for the purposes of the IC review:\n(a) give a decision to which the question is relevant before the\nFederal Court of Australia makes a decision in relation to the\nreference; or\n(b) proceed in a manner, or make a decision, that is inconsistent\nwith the opinion of the Federal Court of Australia on the\nquestion.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-55h-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-55h-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-55h-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-55h-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-55h-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "437cd9e58ccf454430475ee65818b9a856b407ba6aaf5757e38c733ab61501e6"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/freedom-of-information-act-1982#sch-4-sec-55h",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "55H Procedure in IC review—reference of questions of law to",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 145,
      "values:originalText": "Federal Court of Australia\n(1) The Information Commissioner may, at any time during an IC\nreview, refer a question of law arising in an IC review to the\nFederal Court of Australia for decision.\n(2) The Information Commissioner may refer the question of law:\n(a) on the application of a review party; or\n(b) on the Information Commissioner’s initiative.\n(3) The Federal Court of Australia has jurisdiction to hear and\ndetermine a question of law referred to it.\n(4) The jurisdiction of the Federal Court of Australia may be exercised\nby a single judge of the Court.\n(5) If a question of law is referred, the Information Commissioner\nmust not, for the purposes of the IC review:\n(a) give a decision to which the question is relevant before the\nFederal Court of Australia makes a decision in relation to the\nreference; or\n(b) proceed in a manner, or make a decision, that is inconsistent\nwith the opinion of the Federal Court of Australia on the\nquestion."
    },
