    {
      "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-56",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "56 Appeals—appeals to Federal Court of Australia on questions of",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/freedom-of-information-act-1982#sch-4-sec-56"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "freedom-of-information-act-1982.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "freedom-of-information-act-1982.pdf, p.162",
      "values:originalText": "law\n(1) A review party may appeal to the Federal Court of Australia, on a\nquestion of law, from a decision of the Information Commissioner\non an IC review.\n(2) An appeal under this section must be instituted:\n(a) either:\n(i) not later than 28 days after the day a decision under\nsection 55K of the Information Commissioner on an IC\nreview is given to the review party; or\n(ii) within the further time that the Federal Court of\nAustralia allows; and\n(b) in any way that is prescribed by rules of court made under the\nFederal Court of Australia Act 1976.\n(3) The Federal Court of Australia has jurisdiction to hear and\ndetermine appeals instituted under this section.\n(4) The jurisdiction of the Federal Court of Australia under\nsubsection (3) includes jurisdiction to make findings of fact under\nsection 56A.\n(5) The Federal Court of Australia:\n(a) must hear and determine the appeal; and\n(b) may make any order or orders that it thinks appropriate by\nreason of its decision.\n(6) Without limiting subsection (5), the orders that the Federal Court\nof Australia may make include the following:\n(a) an order affirming the decision of the Information\nCommissioner;\n\n\nReview by Information Commissioner  Part VII\nAppeals  Division 10\nSection 56A\nFreedom of Information Act 1982\nCompilation date: 01/01/2026\n(b) an order setting aside the decision of the Information\nCommissioner and making a decision in substitution for the\ndecision;\n(c) an order remitting the case to be considered and decided\nagain by the Information Commissioner in accordance with\nthe directions of the Court:\n(i) with or without the holding of a hearing; and\n(ii) with or without the hearing of further evidence.",
      "skos:definition": "law\n(1) A review party may appeal to the Federal Court of Australia, on a\nquestion of law, from a decision of the Information Commissioner\non an IC review.\n(2) An appeal under this section must be instituted:\n(a) either:\n(i) not later than 28 days after the day a decision under\nsection 55K of the Information Commissioner on an IC\nreview is given to the review party; or\n(ii) within the further time that the Federal Court of\nAustralia allows; and\n(b) in any way that is prescribed by rules of court made under the\nFederal Court of Australia Act 1976.\n(3) The Federal Court of Australia has jurisdiction to hear and\ndetermine appeals instituted under this section.\n(4) The jurisdiction of the Federal Court of Australia under\nsubsection (3) includes jurisdiction to make findings of fact under\nsection 56A.\n(5) The Federal Court of Australia:\n(a) must hear and determine the appeal; and\n(b) may make any order or orders that it thinks appropriate by\nreason of its decision.\n(6) Without limiting subsection (5), the orders that the Federal Court\nof Australia may make include the following:\n(a) an order affirming the decision of the Information\nCommissioner;\n\n\nReview by Information Commissioner  Part VII\nAppeals  Division 10\nSection 56A\nFreedom of Information Act 1982\nCompilation date: 01/01/2026\n(b) an order setting aside the decision of the Information\nCommissioner and making a decision in substitution for the\ndecision;\n(c) an order remitting the case to be considered and decided\nagain by the Information Commissioner in accordance with\nthe directions of the Court:\n(i) with or without the holding of a hearing; and\n(ii) with or without the hearing of further evidence.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-56-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-56-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-56-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-56-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-56-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-act-1982-sch-4-sec-56-ss-6"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "8f1f74adf65864c86d3d471ea8ca9e329d4282489edc156c99604264090774bf"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/freedom-of-information-act-1982#sch-4-sec-56",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "56 Appeals—appeals to Federal Court of Australia on questions of",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 162,
      "values:originalText": "law\n(1) A review party may appeal to the Federal Court of Australia, on a\nquestion of law, from a decision of the Information Commissioner\non an IC review.\n(2) An appeal under this section must be instituted:\n(a) either:\n(i) not later than 28 days after the day a decision under\nsection 55K of the Information Commissioner on an IC\nreview is given to the review party; or\n(ii) within the further time that the Federal Court of\nAustralia allows; and\n(b) in any way that is prescribed by rules of court made under the\nFederal Court of Australia Act 1976.\n(3) The Federal Court of Australia has jurisdiction to hear and\ndetermine appeals instituted under this section.\n(4) The jurisdiction of the Federal Court of Australia under\nsubsection (3) includes jurisdiction to make findings of fact under\nsection 56A.\n(5) The Federal Court of Australia:\n(a) must hear and determine the appeal; and\n(b) may make any order or orders that it thinks appropriate by\nreason of its decision.\n(6) Without limiting subsection (5), the orders that the Federal Court\nof Australia may make include the following:\n(a) an order affirming the decision of the Information\nCommissioner;\n\n\nReview by Information Commissioner  Part VII\nAppeals  Division 10\nSection 56A\nFreedom of Information Act 1982\nCompilation date: 01/01/2026\n(b) an order setting aside the decision of the Information\nCommissioner and making a decision in substitution for the\ndecision;\n(c) an order remitting the case to be considered and decided\nagain by the Information Commissioner in accordance with\nthe directions of the Court:\n(i) with or without the holding of a hearing; and\n(ii) with or without the hearing of further evidence."
    },
